Charlie <choochoocharlie@spamherelots.com> writes:

> Is there ever a circumstance when hallucination is advantageous,
> analogous to the way confusion can be advantageous for reading certain
> scrolls or something like that?

Yes there is.


A source snippet from the function cast_wizard_spell in mcastu.c:

    switch (spellnum) {
    case MGC_DEATH_TOUCH:
        pline("Oh no, %s's using the touch of death!", mhe(mtmp));
        if (nonliving(youmonst.data) || is_demon(youmonst.data)) {
            You("seem no deader than before.");
        } else if (!Antimagic && rn2(mtmp->m_lev) > 12) {
            if (Hallucination) {
                You("have an out of body experience.");
            } else {
                killer_format = KILLED_BY_AN;
                killer = "touch of death";
                done(DIED);
            }
        } else {
            if (Antimagic) shieldeff(u.ux, u.uy);
            pline("Lucky for you, it didn't work!");
        }

This effectively means that there is a situation where you just have an
out of body experience instead of dying when you are hit with the spell of
touch of death.
IIRC, this function is called when an arch-lich is using its spell attack.
And I think the !Antimagic part of the condition around the Hallucination
condition is true when you don't have magic resistance.

